Date: Thu, 23 Dec 1993 10:51:50 -0800 (PST)
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: May's Social Darwinism

Christopher Weeks makes some good comments:

> My recollection and understanding of Tim's posts don't indicate that  
> he thinks the poor deserve their fate.  He just doesn't care.  It's  
> not his (or my) problem that some folks are poor.  It doesn't matter  
> whether they deserve it or not.  

Exactly and precisely (for emphasis). I reject the idea that I have a
legal, moral, or practical responsibility to help, feed, subsidize, or
educate people who are outside my "kin and friends" group. 

The modern tactic of "transferrence" (sp?), whereby one is supposed to
feel that a total stranger, be it Polly Klaas or Baby Jessica
(remember the well) or some starving Sudanese is actually like one's
own child or friend or coworker is the technique that is being used.
The television pictures show thousands of starving Somalis, Bosnians,
Russians, whatever, and we are expected to gush "Aww!" and send them
checks and food baskets.

If it were only charity, no problem (except that I don't want to watch
this crap and so will flick the channel over to the Math Channel to
hear the latest on "Not Knot"). No, the problem is that this
transferrence, which alters the "connectivity" of the social space we
inhabit to make billions of starving peasants our putative
"neighbors," also alters our political and military policies. I cite
the recent adventures in Kuwait, Panama, Somalia, Haiti, and elsewhere
as examples. Only the Kuwait case could be said to have strategic
implications for the United States (not that I care about the U.S. per
se, you understand). The rest were misguided efforts to use coercion
(of me, as a taxpayer, for example) for "humnanitarian" goals.

Humans were not evolved to care deeply about people in distant tribes
and with whom they can expect to have no mutually beneficial relationships.

The Somalis don't give a damn about _us_, why should we give a damn
about them? If others feel differently, fine. But don't steal my money
for your ideas of charity.

> >>Surprise, surprise, Tim Starr thinks I'm bad public relations for  
> >>the Libertarian Cause.
> >
> >I'll bet that a jury of reasonable people will agree with me, too.
> 
> So what?  This isn't a libertarian list.  I refuse to call myself  
> libertarian, due to the perceived ability and rationality of _most_  
> libertarians.  Still, I feel strongly extropian.

That's right. This is a safe haven where we don't have to listen to
the whines of statists and do-gooders (who want to do _their_ good
with _my_ money) and where we can "display our true colors."

It has recently been argued that we should be more persuasive, more
outreaching, more like the Cato Institute. But we're not the Cato
Institute, which does a lot of very fine work, but which also has a
public facade, a staff of media analysts, a public relations machine,
and a staff of "policy advocates" with many years of experience in
contact with the public.

No, we on this List are a fairly young bunch (many of you still in
college, many others just getting started), ill-prepared to start a
public lobbying campaign for our "ideas." (If you want to, go right
ahead. But don't expect to repeat the success of the Cato Institute,
for reasons we can discuss later.)

This list is a safe haven, in many cases for people who gave up on
Libernet in disgust, because Libernet itself attracted the same kind
of wimp-simps we're now seeing here on this list. Folks who ask what
the Scrooge-like libertarians "plan to do" with the poor and
illiterate.

(My answer of course: "Nothing, except to defend myself from them and
their self-appointed advocates.")

> >>>and have no claim even upon our charity, but others are victims,  
> >>>others just plain unlucky, and others prefer leisure to working.
> >>
> >>And none of them have a claim on my time or resources, period.
> >
> >Strawman.  I didn't even imply that they did, nor did Betsy or  
> >anyone else to my knowledge.
> 
> If you aren't comfortable with the word "implied," in the context of  
> the discussion your statement clearly allows for an inference on the  
> part of the reader in the direction of Tim's "strawman."

Any position which argues that social programs (and many or all
others, of course) may need to be continued (rather than stopped
immediately, the shock therapy I advocate) or even expanded in some
ways is effectively a position that says others have a claim on my
resources.

Or do Tim Starr, Betsy Schwartz, and their ilk think taxes are voluntary?

> >>>Then why not go for the Final Solution?  What reason do you have  
> >>>not to?  If they deserve to die, then they ought to be killed.  If  
> >>>they're in your way, then the sooner you have them liquidated, the  
> >>>better.
> >>
> >>???
> >
> >Those are the logical conclusions which follow from the premises you
> >expressed in the post of yours which I replied to, as I would bet a  
> >jury composed of reasonable people would agree.
> 
> No they aren't.

Christopher is completely right here. My arguments that the poor and
clueless have no claims on my resources, and that I basically don't
care what they do with their lives so long as they leave me alone, is
not in any sense a call for their physical extermination.

That Tim Starr would actually make this point shows either his utter
lack of debating skills, his general foolishness, or is in any case a
good example of Godwin's Law ("In all debates about liberty, not more
than 5 days will elapse before Nazis are brought up.")

> >Seemed to me more like May was trying to alienate Betsy from posting  
> >anymore by being downright rude to her.
> 
> This does seem to be one of Tim's tactics sometimes...but there  
