Duncan Frissell writes:

> L.>I consider this unfortunate, as I see much libertarian thinking as 
> L.>necessarily very subtle -- the hand is *invisible*, after all.  Human 
> L.>behavior is complex, which is why attempts to analyze and control it 
> L.>in a simplistic manner (like the centralized planning of socialism)
> L.>have never succeeded for any length of time.  So I believe that we  
> L.>libertarians must master the art of subtle argument if we hope to 
> L.>persuade anybody but ourselves.
> 
> Subtle argument is necessary if you are scoring points on an intellectual 
> forum and approaching the "freedom philosophy" as something that has to be 
> sold.  I don't think that is necessary any longer.  It was always unlikely 
> that the state and its minions would be convinced to wither away by our 
> admittedly piercing logic.

Exactly. And strong messages are often the best way to get a message
across. Look at the blatant propaganda in "Atlas Shrugged"--this was
the strong message that converted many, and I suspect _most_, current
libertarians and Extropians to their present beliefs. Shock therapy,
with no quarter given.

And let's not forget Heinlein, who has an equally important effect. He
was called a Nazi for "Starship Troopers," a misogynist for nearly
everything he wrote, a racist for "Farnham's Freehold," and a
libertarian dreamer for "The Moon is a Harsh Mistress."

Anyone who has not read "Moon" should get if for themselves as a
Christmas present and then have a hell of a good time reading it. As
you read about Manny, Wyoming, the Professor, and Mike, ask yourself
how they'd answer Betsy's question about what libertarians plan to do
about the poor.

(By the way, of these four main characters, one is black, one is a
woman, one is a computer, and only one is a "white male." Score
another one against Heinlein's critics.)

Anyway, my point is that strong views, clear views, are often needed.
Watering down our message so that we appear to be some cross between
the Futurist Society, the Cato Institute, and The League of Women
Voters is not going to do it.

Duncan continues:

> I prefer playing hardball these days and just informing people that they 
> and everyone else around them are freeish and getting freer all the time 
> and that they better hope that libertarians are right because none of the 
> *other* social systems can cope with relatively powerful individuals.  

I fully agree.

> All the other systems (except maybe some of Buddhism and Christian sects 
> like the Society of Friends) require that some authority deliver realistic 
> death threats to those who fail to obey.  That requires a massive 
> imbalance of power that is fading away as technology multiplies our 
> individual capabilities.  
> 
> There is only one system possibly capable of (more or less) coping with 
> powerful, autonomous, brainy, and vicious primates -- markets and 
> contracts.

And Extropians and Cypherpunks are in a position to potentially profit
from this phase change.

We can best help "the poor" by setting an example and protection
ourselves against predation so as to remove any temptation they may
have to simply _take_ what they want.

This doesn't mean I'll try to exterminated them in a Final
Solution...in fact, if they have something of value, I'll willingly
trade with them. 

"Extremism in defense of virtue is no vice."

--Tim May


